# NestJS patterns

Module/provider/request-pipeline design for NestJS (official NestJS
documentation) and its recurring anti-patterns. Applies only to `*.ts` files
in a NestJS project; general TypeScript design guidance comes from
`ts-js-node`'s own rules.

## Module and provider design

- A feature module owns its controllers, services, and their providers;
  imports other feature modules only for providers it consumes, exports only
  what others should inject.
- Break a real circular dependency with `forwardRef()` on both sides, but
  first reconsider the boundary; prefer a shared third module both depend on.
- Constructor injection over property injection (`@Inject()` on a field)
  except a truly optional dependency with `@Optional()`.
- Default (Singleton) scope for stateless services; `Scope.REQUEST` only when
  the provider needs per-request state (e.g. the `REQUEST` token), since it
  re-instantiates its injection subtree per request.

## Guards, interceptors, pipes, and filters

- Cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, response shaping, validation) go in
  a guard/interceptor/pipe/filter, not inline per controller method.
- Register a concern that applies to nearly every route as a global provider
  via `APP_GUARD`/`APP_INTERCEPTOR`/`APP_PIPE`/`APP_FILTER` in a module's
  `providers` (inside Nest's DI graph, unlike `app.useGlobalGuards()`), not
  by decorating every controller.
- When a global guard protects most routes, mark exceptions with a custom
  decorator read via `Reflector` (e.g. `@Public()`); an undecorated route
  must not be silently open via the guard's fallback.
- A guard only decides allow/deny, an interceptor transforms/observes the
  request-response stream, a pipe transforms/validates one argument; a
  decision needing several service calls moves to the service layer, which
  the guard calls.

## Controllers vs. services

- A controller method parses/validates input (DTO + `ValidationPipe`),
  delegates to exactly one service call, and shapes the HTTP response; no
  direct repository/ORM calls, business-rule branching, or orchestration of
  several service calls a service method could compose.
- A service method has an explicit return type and throws a Nest HTTP
  exception (see `rules/coding-style.mdc`) or a domain error the caller
  translates; it imports `Request`/`Response` only for a documented
  streaming/file-download case.

## Anti-patterns to avoid

- Injecting `ModuleRef` or `moduleRef.get(Provider, { strict: false })` to
  route around a missing `imports`/`exports` entry; fix the module graph.
- A `useValue`/`useFactory` provider whose factory acquires a resource (e.g.
  opens a connection) without a matching `OnModuleDestroy` hook.
- Role/permission rules as scattered per-endpoint `@Roles()`/`@Permissions()`
  strings with no single source of truth; centralize the matrix and have a
  guard read it.
